<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyrie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">a biblical phrase; sung in the liturgy, in different ways East and West, by the 6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Κύριε ἐλέησον, Kýrie eléison</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyrie is the Greek word for &quot;Lord&quot; in the form used to address someone, and it gives its name to one of the important prayers of Christian liturgy, the Kyrie eleison, &quot;Lord, have mercy&quot;. The prayer comes from the Bible. A plea for mercy addressed to the Lord appears often in the Psalms (6:2, 9:13, 31:9, 86:3 and 123:3), and the Greek words are those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>.</p></div>
